LRRS ? PHASE 3 COMPATIBILITY ANALYSIS
======================================================================

CONTEXT
-------
Phase 3 will implement a counterfactual risk-reduction engine.
Given a borrower with a predicted default probability above a threshold,
Phase 3 will evaluate alternative loan configurations (different loan amount,
term, or rate) to find the configuration that minimises predicted default
probability while remaining feasible.

This document identifies which features can be modified, which cannot,
and what constraints Phase 3 must respect.

?? 1. ACTIONABLE LOAN VARIABLES ??
These can realistically be changed by modifying the loan offer.

  Variable:     loan_amnt
  Description:  Requested loan amount
  Range/Notes:  Can be decreased (LC allows partial offers). Reducing loan_amnt decreases installment and loan_to_income.
  Constraints:  Must remain positive. LC minimum ~$1,000.
  Downstream:   installment changes (derived from loan_amnt, term, int_rate), loan_to_income changes, installment_to_income changes, total_debt_load changes, log_loan_amnt changes

  Variable:     term
  Description:  Loan repayment term in months
  Range/Notes:  Only two values in LendingClub: 36 or 60 months.
  Constraints:  Binary choice ? cannot be set to arbitrary values.
  Downstream:   installment changes (longer term ? lower monthly payment), installment_to_income changes, term_int_interaction changes, total_debt_load changes

  Variable:     int_rate
  Description:  Interest rate assigned at origination
  Range/Notes:  Set by lender based on risk tier. In counterfactual analysis, can model what-if the rate were lower.
  Constraints:  In practice, determined by grade/creditworthiness. Must remain ? 0. Changing rate without changing grade creates an inconsistency that Phase 3 must handle carefully.
  Downstream:   installment changes, int_rate_spread changes (deviation from grade mean), term_int_interaction changes

  Variable:     installment
  Description:  Monthly installment amount
  Range/Notes:  DERIVED from loan_amnt, term, int_rate. Should NOT be independently modified ? it will be recalculated from the other actionable variables.
  Constraints:  Dependent variable ? always computed from loan terms.
  Downstream:   installment_to_income changes, total_debt_load changes

?? 2. NON-ACTIONABLE VARIABLES ??
These describe the borrower and cannot be changed.

  annual_inc                               Borrower's income ? cannot be changed by loan configuration.
  dti                                      Debt-to-income ? reflects existing obligations, not loan terms.
  emp_length                               Employment history ? cannot be changed.
  home_ownership                           Home ownership status ? cannot be changed.
  delinq_2yrs                              Past delinquency record ? cannot be changed.
  pub_rec                                  Public records ? cannot be changed.
  revol_bal                                Existing revolving balance ? not controlled by this loan.
  revol_util                               Revolving utilisation ? reflects existing credit usage.
  open_acc                                 Open accounts ? historical, cannot be changed.
  total_acc                                Total accounts ? historical, cannot be changed.
  inq_last_6mths                           Hard inquiries ? historical, cannot be changed.
  grade                                    LC-assigned credit grade ? derived from creditworthiness, not a free variable.
  sub_grade                                LC-assigned sub-grade ? same as grade.
  credit_age_months                        Credit history length ? cannot be changed.
  log_annual_inc                           Log transform of income ? non-actionable.
  revol_bal_to_income                      Existing debt ratio ? non-actionable.
  open_acc_ratio                           Historical credit behaviour ? non-actionable.
  delinq_flag                              Binary delinquency indicator ? non-actionable.
  pub_rec_flag                             Binary public record indicator ? non-actionable.
  inq_burden                               Inquiry count ? non-actionable.

?? 3. MISSING VARIABLES (not in LendingClub) ??
Important credit-risk variables absent from the dataset.

  collateral_value                         LendingClub is unsecured lending ? no collateral data.
  credit_score_raw                         Only grade/sub_grade proxy available, not raw FICO band in all versions.
  existing_emi_obligations                 Total monthly EMI from other loans not directly available.
  income_stability_score                   No direct employment type (salaried vs. self-employed) flag.
  savings_balance                          No savings/liquid assets data.
  co_borrower_income                       Only joint application flag; co-borrower income only for joint loans.

?? 4. CRITICAL CONSTRAINTS FOR PHASE 3 ??

(a) INSTALLMENT IS DERIVED ? NOT INDEPENDENTLY ACTIONABLE.
    installment = f(loan_amnt, term, int_rate)
    Phase 3 must recalculate installment whenever it changes any of:
    loan_amnt, term, or int_rate.
    Changing installment directly while keeping loan_amnt fixed would
    create an inconsistent loan configuration.

(b) TERM IS BINARY IN LENDINGCLUB.
    Only 36 or 60 months. Phase 3 must enumerate both options, not
    treat term as a continuous variable.

(c) GRADE AND INT_RATE ARE CORRELATED.
    In LendingClub, int_rate is largely determined by grade/sub_grade.
    If Phase 3 changes int_rate independently, it may create
    configurations where int_rate is inconsistent with grade.
    Approach: either (i) hold grade fixed and modify only loan_amnt and term,
    or (ii) treat int_rate as a direct controllable and accept that
    grade may become a stale feature.

(d) REALISTIC RANGES.
    loan_amnt: $1,000 ? $40,000 (LendingClub limits)
    term: {36, 60}
    int_rate: typically 5% ? 30% depending on grade

(e) MONOTONICITY ASSUMPTIONS.
    Lower loan amount ? lower installment ? lower installment_to_income
    ? generally lower predicted default risk.
    This is not guaranteed by the model, so Phase 3 must evaluate
    predictions empirically rather than assuming monotone relationships.

?? 5. RECOMMENDED PHASE 3 APPROACH ??

1. Fix all non-actionable features (borrower characteristics).
2. Enumerate a grid of (loan_amnt, term) combinations.
3. For each combination, recompute derived features:
     installment, loan_to_income, installment_to_income,
     total_debt_load, log_loan_amnt, term_int_interaction.
4. Keep int_rate either fixed (hold-constant) or modelled as a
   function of loan_amnt and grade (regression sub-model).
5. Pass each configuration through the calibrated model.
6. Return the configuration with the lowest predicted default
   probability that still satisfies business constraints.

