9. COST-SENSITIVE THRESHOLD ANALYSIS
????????????????????????????????????????
  FN/FP cost ratio: 5.0
  Rationale: Missing a defaulter (FN) costs ~5? more than
             flagging a safe borrower (FP).
             A missed default ? full principal loss.
             A false alarm ? lost interest margin on one loan.
  RF optimal threshold:   0.2464
  XGB optimal threshold:  0.1725

10. BEST BASELINE MODEL
????????????????????????

  RECOMMENDATION: XGBoost

  XGBoost achieves higher PR-AUC (the primary metric for imbalanced default
  prediction). It also typically produces better-calibrated probability
  estimates for this class of financial dataset. XGBoost handles the
  interaction structure between grade, int_rate, and loan features
  efficiently through its gradient-boosted tree structure.

  Random Forest remains a strong ensemble baseline and is useful as a
  comparison point. For Phase 2+, XGBoost's calibrated probabilities
  are recommended as the primary risk score.

11. ACTIONABLE VARIABLES FOR PHASE 3 RISK REDUCTION
??????????????????????????????????????????????????

  Variable          Notes
  ?????????????????????????????????????????????????
  loan_amnt         Primary actionable variable. Reducing loan amount
                    directly reduces installment and loan_to_income.

  term              Binary (36 or 60 months). Longer term reduces
                    monthly installment but increases total interest paid.

  int_rate          Set by lender based on risk tier. Can be modelled
                    as a controllable variable with caveats (grade consistency).

  installment       DERIVED from above ? recalculated by Phase 3,
                    not independently modified.

  CRITICAL: installment = loan_amnt ? [rate/12 ? (1+rate/12)^n] / [(1+rate/12)^n - 1]
  Phase 3 must use this formula to recalculate installment when changing
  loan_amnt, term, or int_rate.

12. POTENTIAL ISSUES FOR PHASE 2 AND PHASE 3
??????????????????????????????????????????????????

  (a) GRADE / INT_RATE CORRELATION
      grade and int_rate are strongly correlated (grade determines rate range).
      Changing int_rate independently in Phase 3 without adjusting grade
      creates an inconsistent feature vector. Recommended approach:
      hold grade constant and treat int_rate as the controllable within-grade knob.

  (b) TEMPORAL SHIFT
      Models trained on 2007?2018 data may not generalise well to post-2018
      credit conditions (COVID, interest rate changes, etc.).
      Phase 2 should evaluate domain shift if newer data is available.

  (c) TERM IS BINARY
      LendingClub only offers 36 or 60-month loans. Phase 3's optimisation
      space is discrete for term, not continuous.

  (d) FEATURE SPARSITY IN JOINT APPLICATIONS
      Joint application fields (annual_inc_joint, dti_joint, etc.) have
      high missingness for individual applications. Imputed with median ?
      Phase 2 should validate joint vs. individual applicant treatment.

  (e) GRADE MAY NOT BE KNOWN AT APPLICATION TIME (marginal)
      LC-assigned grade is technically determined after application review.
      In practice it is part of the application-time package offered to
      investors before funding. It is retained as an application-time feature
      but Phase 2 should be aware of this nuance.

  (f) CALIBRATED PROBABILITIES ON TEST SET
      The test set was used ONLY for final evaluation, not for calibration
      fitting. Calibration was fitted on the validation set. This is correct
      methodology but means the reported Brier improvement is measured on
      the validation set; test set Brier should be the primary reference.

